{"content":[{"c":["EVIL-MERODACH"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Son and successor of Nebuchadnezzar as king of Babylon, who reigned for two years (561–560 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"},"). During his reign, he released Jehoiachin, former king of Judah, from imprisonment (",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.25.27-30","r":"2Kgs.25.27-30","rawR":"2Kgs.25.27-30","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.25.27-30","value":"2Kgs.25.27-30"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 25:27-30"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.52.31-34","r":"Jer.52.31-34","rawR":"Jer.52.31-34","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.52.31-34","value":"Jer.52.31-34"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 52:31-34"},"). Aside from this fact, little is known about his reign. He was killed by his brother-in-law Neriglissar, who succeeded him to the throne. ",{"c":["See"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=BabylonBabylonia_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"BabylonBabylonia_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"BabylonBabylonia_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Babylon, Babylonia"},"."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"EvilMerodach","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"2 Kgs 25:27-30","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=2Kgs.25.27-30","rawQueryValue":"2Kgs.25.27-30","sourceRefExact":"2Kgs.25.27-30"},{"anchorText":"Jer 52:31-34","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Jer.52.31-34","rawQueryValue":"Jer.52.31-34","sourceRefExact":"Jer.52.31-34"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"df05f42ffdb95ccef32a4c9879cb46cb745102da80ad9ccd4ae1e55af39a40cd","sourceItemName":"EvilMerodach","sourceItemOrder":287,"sourceMember":"Articles/E.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"EvilMerodach","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTi